      "text": "Let's denote Cyprian's scores as \\( C_1, C_2, C_3 \\) and Margaret's scores as \\( M_1, M_2, M_3 \\). We know that Cyprian's mean score is 90, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{C_1 + C_2 + C_3}{3} = 90\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\n\\[\nC_1 + C_2 + C_3 = 270\n\\]\n\nWe also know the following scores: 85, 87, 92, 93, 94, 98. Since Cyprian's scores sum to 270, the remaining three scores must be:\n\n\\[\n98, 94, 92\n\\]\n\nLet's denote Margaret's scores as \\( M_1, M_2, M_3 \\) such that the sum of Margaret's scores is:\n\n\\[\nM_1 + M_2 + M_3 = \\text{unknown}\n\\]\n\nGiven the sum of the scores, we can infer the sum of Cyprian's scores:\n\n\\[\n85 + 87 + 92 = 264\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n98 + 94 + 92 = 284\n\\]\n\nThe sum of all six scores is:\n\n\\[\n264 + 284 = 548\n\\]\n\nHence, the sum of Margaret's scores is \\( 548 - 270 = 278 \\).\n\nTo find Margaret's mean score, we divide the sum of her scores by 3:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{278}{3} = 92.67\n\\]\n\nThus, Margaret's mean score is:\n\n\\boxed{92.67}",
